<p>Whenever anybody mentions AI (artificial intelligence), it always sounds super-cool and we should definitely do it.  It's a bit like building the Channel Tunnel or HS2: if you don't agree with doing it, you must be some kind of killjoy.</p>
<p>So, here we go.  It's not so much "I don't agree with doing it" as "I don't think you even know what you think you are trying to achieve, so how can you measure your progress?"  Yes, I'm a killjoy.  I think we are nowhere.</p>
<p>I don't know how long AI has been a thing, but obviously it's a staple of Science Fiction going back to at least 1930; Alan Turing described the  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turing_test'>Turing Test</a> to determine if an artificial device could be considered intelligent before he died in 1954; and it's my understanding  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_McCarthy_(computer_scientist)'>John McCarthy</a> originally invented LISP in 1959 to support his agenda for pursuing artificial intelligence based on the  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dartmouth_workshop'>seminal work in 1956</a>.</p>
<p>OK, that makes AI one of a handful of things in Computer Science we still talk about which are older than I am. Aside from all the "spin off" technology (I believe that the humble mouse, invented at Xerox PARC, was spun off from a project at the Stanford AI Lab, but I have been told that anecdotally and cannot find a reference), I question how much progress we have made in the last 70 years.</p>
<h2><p>What We Expect
</h2>
<p>I think most of us have a shared vision of "what AI is" - possibly to the extent that we don't ask ourselves even if our belief is rational.  To state it briefly, we expect to sit in a comfy chair, say a few words and have our spaceship take off and go wherever we intended.</p>
<p>Let's break that down a little bit to investigate all the moving parts.</p>
<p>We expect to be able to talk to a computer, which means that it must be able to hear what we say and understand the natural language (English in my case) that we speak.  It must have concepts that match the words that we use and be able to build a model of what it wants us to do.  It then needs to interface with whatever gadgets exist (whether spaceships or something more mundane like a calendar or music player) and carry out our instructions.</p>
<p>Said like that, it almost sounds plausible.  And keep the applications simple enough, and, as Siri and Alexa have shown, it can be useful.  But let's consider some additional cases that were just glossed over.  In all these cases, I do the simple step of imagining what I would do - as a moderately intelligent "natural" intelligence - in the same situation.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>It might not have realized you were talking to it.  We all do this; there is a lot of chatter going on, and even if someone says your name, you may not realize they were talking to you.  There needs to be some protocol in place where you can definitely get it to pay attention and confirm that it is doing so.</p>
<li><p>It might not have been able to figure out what you said.  Again, we all have this, and particuarly when we are talking over the phone or in a pub.  If there is distortion or a lot of background noise, we may not be able to hear what was said or figure it out.  We need to be able to see what we can figure out and then ask for a repetition or clarification.</p>
<li><p>What you said may not have "made sense".  Presumably it made sense to you, but somewhere between your mental model and the computer's, the words you used did not end up making the same sense in the computer's head that it did in yours.  Depending on <i>how</i> far off it is, it might be possible to check between alternatives, to ask for context, to ask clarifying questions, or to just ask for you to explain yourself more clearly.  This definitely happens with call centre phone systems when they say "just say anything" and then the next thing is "I didn't understand that".  Hopefully followed by "I'll transfer you to someone who can help".</p>
<li><p>It's possible that you have not been precise enough.  "Take me home" may seem to have an unambiguous meaning, but consider how many times in your life you have said it while on holiday.  In that context, you mean "take me back to the hotel".  I don't think Google Maps would know that.  But I would expect anything claiming to be intelligent to at least ask the question.</p>
<li><p>Beyond that, there may be other things which we often assume we know the answers to, but sometimes we need to check:  "Do you want to go on the motorway or take the scenic route?"</p>
</ul>
<p>So, what do we want AI to do?</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Listen to us</p>
<li><p>Interact with us in our language and understand what we want, asking (probing) questions as necessary</p>
<li><p>Formulate a plan</p>
<li><p>Interact with other physical or digital systems to produce the desired result</p>
</ul>
<p>This is actually all a lot more complicated than it might at first appear.</p>
<h2><p>A Brief History of AI
</h2>
<p>I'm not going to cover everything, because this is anything but my area of expertise.  Inasmuch as I <i>have</i> thought about it, or had anything to do with it, I will cover that in the next section.</p>
<p>In order to make a plan for implementing artificial intelligence, you need to first figure out how you think natural intelligence works, so that you can copy it.  This is hindered by two salient facts:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>There are a lot of people who are "supersititous" about it (for want of a better word) and believe that consciousness is something actually more complex that processing and cannot be imitated;</p>
<li><p>The technology of our brains is incredibly complex and incredibly cunning and has taken the best part of 2 billion years to develop.  While we think we are super smart because of all the innovations we have made over the past 100 years, it really is a drop in the ocean.  It is not unreasonable to think that we need a massive (1000x? More?) increase in computing power in order to emulate that.  And then we have to use that <i>efficiently</i>.  If we don't, you can throw in another 1000x.</p>
</ul>
<p>I haven't really done any research, so I'm going to talk about what I have "bumped into" through my life and career.</p>
<p>It seems to me that the first models of artificial intelligence were based on the idea that humans are logical, and that there are inputs and stimuli that lead to responses and outputs.  We can copy this by writing down all the rules that are in our brains and then applying them to incoming data.</p>
<p>As far as I can tell, this is what everybody did with Lisp and Prolog in the 60s, 70s and 80s and thought they were achieving something with toys like the  <a href='https://skolemmachines.org/ThePrologTutorial/www/prolog_tutorial/2_17.html'>animal identification system</a> (this is now hard to find in Google because there are much more modern, and useful, photo recognition apps available).  At the time, I could not figure out if they were much smarter than I was, or if they were fooling themselves.  My instinct was to agree with Douglas Adams' critique in  <a href=''>Dirk Gently's Holistic Detective Agency</a> where he says something to the effect that "we need to consider the possibility that AI based on the ProLog language is a pipe dream" (much of the book is a good source of how people thought about advanced computing in those days).</p>
<p>The problem, of course, is that the human brain is <b>capable</b> of doing formal reasoning, but it isn't actually very good at it.  It is not surprising then that a system that <i>is</i> doing formal reasoning is unlikely to simulate the brain very well.  To misquote Douglas Adams again (<i>ibid</i>), "people who can't even do basic algebra are able to solve differential equations quickly enough <i>to catch a flying ball</i>".  Humans are clearly doing something very different to thinking about problems.</p>
<p>Around the end of the 1980s, Neural Networks came on the scene.  This was an idea based on a better understanding of how the brain actually works: it is a set of (fairly) simple neurons, which are connected to (thousands of) other neurons in an environment with about  <a href='https://hms.harvard.edu/news/new-field-neuroscience-aims-map-connections-brain'>86 billion</a> total neurons and around 100 trillion connections.  For comparison, the first example "neural networks" I saw would fit on a piece of paper and have about 10 nodes and 20 connections. It was just about enough to react to a simulated light flashing by generating an output and then turning it off after a while.</p>
<p>But the fundamental problem with neural networks as a solution is  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mind%E2%80%93body_problem'>the "mind/body" problem</a>.  Basically, there is some thinking going on, and then some actions take place.  These are disconnected.  I first came across this in a  <a href='https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/262009.262011'>paper on functional programming and monads</a> by Phil Wadler where he discussed the fact that Descartes believed the  <a href='https://www.neurology.org/doi/10.1212/WNL.94.15_supplement.914'>pineal gland</a> was a link between "thoughts" and "actions".  In other words, the mind decided what it wanted to do, sent commands to the pineal gland, and this then executed them.  His proposal for monads in Haskell followed the same plot, and I have used much the same technique many times (always calling my class <tt>Pineal</tt>) to connect a <i>declarative</i> to a real world impact.  This is of course not at all what happens, and the central nervous system is connected to the "actual" body all over the place and there is not a single place "where it all comes together".  Over and over again in the history of computer science, this thought trips us up, that the world is linear because it "feels" that way inside our brains.  It's not.  Get over it.</p>
<p>The real world is massively, massively parallel.  They say (some?) dinosaurs had four brains, and this feels weird, until you read that in reality we have two: the processing of food into nutrients is so complex that we have a specialized brain in our intestines for dealing with that exact problem.  Like our main brain, it uses a lot of energy, so we are naturally attempted to conserve energy while digesting food by <i>turning the other one off</i> - or having a nap as I like to call it.</p>
<p>If you have ever watched a child learning to walk, you will have seen this process in action.  They do not think "I will move this leg, then that leg" but the whole process takes months - from learning that they have control of their legs, to learning to pull themselves upright, to moving their feet while holding on to a solid object, to finally taking a few steps themselves before they fall over.  This is not them honing each of a set of processes that are abstractly connected, but a <i>fully integrated machine</i> learning with feedback.  To even <b>think</b> about this as being a command-and-control environment is to so completely misunderstand "the problem" that you stand <b>no chance</b> of "solving" it.</p>
<p>I imagine people kept on working on this, and this "deep AI" is probably behind the recent innovations with Large Language Models which we'll come back to in a bit.</p>
<p>But in the meantime, the software industry realized that there were a lot of "real world" problems that you could solve without solving either "deep AI" or the mind/body problem.  Indeed, I believe that the phrase "deep AI" was coined to distinguish it from these "cheap tricks".</p>
<p>When I was working at Bristol University (more on this later), my main focus was on programming languages and proof.  But there were teams working with AI (mainly in Prolog), graphics and multiprocessor systems (anybody out there remember the  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transputer'>transputer</a>?), and vision research.  For reasons I won't bore you with, I ended up being involved with all of these at one time or another.  In particular, the vision team "won" two research contracts: one was to investigate self-driving cars (yes, in 1990!) and the other was what became  <a href='https://www.police.uk/advice/advice-and-information/rs/road-safety/automatic-number-plate-recognition-anpr/'>ANPR</a>.</p>
<p>Now, as someone who tends to like things precise and nailed down, all of this AI stuff seems fairly "seat of the pants", but the work that "we" did on ANPR was cool in a number of ways.  One was the genuinely iterative nature of the process. There were three categories that could come out of a scan: "nothing there", "cannot read" or an actual result.  In each case, there was an opportunity for the system to improve:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If it said there was nothing there, but a human scanning the image could see a plate, it could indicate where the plate was, possibly suggesting some filters and controls, and then indicate what the plate said (assuming they could tell);</p>
<li><p>If it said that it could not read the plate, the human could give the correct answer;</p>
<li><p>If it gave an answer, the human could give it another answer.</p>
</ul>
<p>The system worked in what I would describe as the "obvious" way. First, we did some general "vision" stuff to clean up the image: removed noise, sharpened lines, looked for contrast boundaries, converted greyscale to black and white, etc.  Then we used a series of heuristics to try and find something that looked like a number plate.  While the heuristics themselves were hardcoded, they could be configured with different parameters.  The feedback loop suggested different ways to apply the heuristics.  Multiple configurations could be tried (I don't remember if there was a voting system, but there easily could have been).  Then we isolated and transformed the number plate so that it was correctly aligned in two dimensions.  Finally, a series of heuristics were used to pick out the individual characters on the number plate and then identify them.  Put them together and you have a number plate.</p>
<p>The key thing here is that <i>no matter how much code we wrote</i>, it was all configurable with different parameters that could be tweaked.  A suitably trained human could look at an image that the computer could not identify and show it where it was going wrong.  By <i>manually</i> tweaking all the configuration parameters, it could get the <i>machine</i> to a point where it could read the number plate for itself.  And then if that configuration was added back into the "bank" of configurations, the machine could automatically find that configuration and read the number plate.</p>
<p>"Is this AI?" I hear you ask.  It depends on your definition of AI, I guess.  But it is very definitely a machine that is learning and can solve problems that on the face of it "need a human" to solve.</p>
<p>Fifteen years later, and in the mid-2000s recommendation engines were everywhere.  These work on a similar principle, but generally using  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayesian_programming'>Bayesian engines</a> instead of a more intuitive model.  The problem - for me, at least - is that once you delegate something to a neural network or a Bayesian engine, you no longer have a clue what is going on.  You put some data in, and you then tweak a button, and something comes out.  It has "learnt" but it cannot tell you what it has learnt.  This is exactly like a child learning to walk - they have learnt a skill but could not tell you afterwards what they have learnt.  Is this "more AI" than what I described above?  In some sense yes, but I still think what it is doing is "less cool": I can easily imagine - and indeed, describe - ways of doing the same thing in which I do not lose sight of my inferences, but can tell you how I reached a conclusion.</p>
<p>Possibly the most powerful learning engine in the world is the Google Search Engine.  I don't claim to know how this works, but I am reliably informed that one of the things that makes it so good is that it learns from what people select.  That is, when you click on a result in a Google search, that is used to train the search engine that that result is the one you want, and it will (I believe) bump that result up (a little) for everyone else too.</p>
<p>Finally we get to the modern era and we have self-driving cars and OpenAI.  There are so many ways of building a self-driving car that I don't even want to go there (especially since I have had no practical experience since 1990).  I also have no firsthand experience of OpenAI - just what I see others report.</p>
<p>It seems to me to be an engine designed to pass the Turing Test: that is, if you are involved in a conversation with it, can you tell if it is human or not?  Since, as I noted above, this has been a yardstick for "AI" since 1950, it's a reasonable goal if you are doing research into AI.  But there is a flipside: we are all becoming (and accepting other people being) so dumb that it is becoming harder and harder to tell.  In particular, could you tell if you were talking to OpenAI or Donald Trump?  In fact, I have often wondered if Trump's famous  <a href='https://time.com/3923128/donald-trump-announcement-speech/'>presidential announcement speech</a> in 2015 was written by an early version of ChatGPT.  Before you start wondering if I've been replaced by AI, let me explain.</p>
<p>Everything I have read about ChatGPT agrees on one thing: it reads voraciously, collates everything, and then regurgitates what it has in a way that generates "new" content that is an extrapolation of everything it has read.  Ask yourself: what else did Trump do in 2015?  He took all the talking points of the extreme right wing groups and synthesized them into a (coherent?) speech making those same points.  I actually think ChatGPT might have spoken in more complete sentences.</p>
<p>What ChatGPT seems <b>unable</b> to do is to apply any critical thinking to the information it reads, or to understand there are factors such as time, place and context which affect what humans say or write.  This came into crystalline focus for me a while back, when I read  <a href='https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/feb/07/digested-week-toast-to-brexit-trump-artificial-intelligence-mornings'>this</a> by the excellent  <a href='https://www.theguardian.com/profile/johncrace'>John Crace</a>:</p>
