<p>Why would it think the Tories were still in government?  Had it not read about the election of last July?  While it's possible it hadn't, since training models takes time, let's assume it had.  Even so, John has been writing sketches for ten years or so now, and during 90% of that time, <i>the Tories were in power</i>.  So it is going to have a 90% probability of casting a Tory as a minister.  Why wouldn't it?  When you see that, you realize there are many  <a href='https://www.shakespeare-online.com/quickquotes/quickquotehamletdreamt.html'>more things going on in your brain, Horatio, than are dreamt of in your philosophy</a>.</p>
<p>And I claim that you can't hope to have anything worthy of the name "artificial intelligence" if you don't do all of them.</p>
<h2><p>My Personal Experiences
</h2>
<p>I spoke briefly above about things that were going on around me when I was at Bristol University which I was dragged into, generally because I could understand frameworks, solve technical problems and write "hard" code.  But my involvement was at best tangential, and I wasn't really grappling with the problem of "cognition".</p>
<p>What I was doing was focusing on programming languages and program proof.  And a key (the irreducible?) problem in computer science is forming concepts and coming up with names for them.</p>
<p>This is often just described as "naming things", but once again, it misses a <i>key</i> step in the process: what are you naming?  You need to first understand the concept of what you are doing; only then can you try and come up with a name for it.  In programming languages, the concepts are at least easier to come by: you can look at the code and decide either what it does or what it needs to do.</p>
<p>So, this seems a good point to go into an aside on the subject of naming things.  What would happen if we put a ban on the phrase "artificial intelligence" and said to everybody working in the field, "tell me what it is you do".  I think a number of answers would come back.  I've tried to group them as best I can and label them with phrases that (hopefully) would make sense to a lay person while also capturing the essence of what each approach is:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>"language models" seems like a good term to describe people who are working with parsing and generating natural language.  This is not quite as simple as parsing a computer language, because everything in a natural language is many-layered and cultural.  Two classic examples are the difference between "time <i>flies</i> like an arrow" and "fruit flies <i>like</i> a banana"; and "colourless green ideas sleep furiously".  The first indicates that two sentences which appear syntactically identical can actually be very different (I have put the verb in italics in each case to make this clear); and the second indicates that even a syntactically valid sentence can be meaningless.  I believe that ChatGPT would "understand" both of these things.</p>
<li><p>"automated problem solver" would describe something that can tackle problems and, given enough data, "think" its way through a problem.  Examples of this might be things such as a fraud detection system at a bank, or a computer system for detecting diseases given sufficient raw data (e.g. CT scans and blood tests).  In all these cases, the system probably has a hybrid of heuristics and learning from feedback from making mistakes.  My description of the trial ANPR system above is a good example of this.</p>
<li><p>"graph resolver" would describe any system which is able to look at a whole bunch of "similar" items (such as books) and spot connections between them.  The obvious reason for doing this is to make recommendations to people, but it could also be useful for capacity planning (do we have enough books on hand given the trends we are seeing).</p>
<li><p>"predictor" is a similar concept: given what we have seen in the past, and given a current situation, how do we think it will fall out?  I will discuss this in more detail below on "university retention".</p>
<li><p>"cognition" describes the process where a system develops a deep understanding about a domain; not so much about the language used to describe it, but about how the domain actually functions.  I will describe this below in more detail with regard to programming assistants.</p>
</ul>
<p>So, there I was, down in the weeds of a functional programming language, trying to build features that would enable me to prove that a program was correct.  Why was I doing this?  Well, it certainly wasn't because I liked program proofs.</p>
<p>(Sorry to go off on another aside, but I do feel the need to mention that I personally <i>never</i> got along with formal program proofs either.  The big problem there is that you make one abstract statement and then compare it to some abstract code and prove that they are the same.  Two things: were <i>either</i> of them what the user wanted? If the first one contains all the knowledge you need, shouldn't <i>that</i> be the program?)</p>
<p>In 1985, having been programming for five years, I had realized that it was hard.  And, annoyingly, a lot of the bits that were hard were also no fun.  And, worse than that, having programmed in assembler for most of that time, I was disturbed that so-called "high level languages" (C and Pascal) didn't actually solve most of my problems (neither did C++, by the way).</p>
<p>I distinctly remember sitting in Brigham's on Mass Ave in Boston, MA (sadly no longer there) in the summer of 1986 and thinking about this.  I'd read an article in  <a href='https://www.computing.co.uk/'>Computing magazine</a> (wow, it's still going?) which had talked about tools to make programming easier and could it be done on a "show me" basis.  I think they were actually just talking about assembling UI using drag-n-drop, which would have been revolutionary at the time (when did MS Windows 1.0 even come out?) but it made me think about everything else.  How would I go about it?  And the obvious answer is the same way a client would go about it: they would find a software professional, describe their problem, answer any questions, wait until there was something to look at, try it out, offer feedback, wait for the next iteration, try it out, offer feedback ...</p>
<p>Why couldn't a computer system do that?  How would it work?  I fairly promptly dismissed natural language, but also dismissed anything that looked too much like "programming".  I approached my project supervisor to discuss and he suggested I look into functional programming with an angle to program proof.  The idea being that a program proof is not <i>too</i> far removed from an explanation of what a program is doing.  Well, actually it is.  Three years later I had a Ph.D. and a thick thesis I still flick through from time to time, but I didn't have wanted I wanted: a <i>programming</i> assistant rather than a <i>proof</i> assistant.  (It did all help me to get excellent, well-paying jobs, so I shouldn't complain too much.)</p>
<p>I have never let go of this idea during the rest of my career, although no matter how many times I have tried, I have never made any serious progress.  But the idea remains the same: there is a program which <i>interacts</i> with you, asking you what you want, allowing you to draw, drag-n-drop, design an interface and then "talk" about what should happen and when.  Internally, it translates all of that into a complex, three-dimensional graph representing "concepts" and the links between them; and then it has a mechanism for spewing out code in a suitable programming language.  Said language being specifically designed to be easy to generate this kind of code for.  As I write this, it seems so easy I want to have another go.  It's not.</p>
<p>This is what I mean by "cognition".  The system needs to have a basic set of knowledge about the world (in this case, it needs to know about the basic building blocks of programming and the language it is trying to generate).  It then needs to have some kind of user interface which enables it to interact with humans.  It needs to be able to interpret this interaction in the context of the knowledge it already has (whether internal or built up from previous interactions) and then it needs to augment its internal knowledge with the products of this interaction.  None of this is easy.</p>
<p>I also did work in the 2010s with "machine learning".  Part of a big project I was involved with was an attempt to build a system that would identify students in danger of dropping out of University and to enable them to access the support they needed in order to address their problems.  The logic went as follows: if we build this big system which measures all sorts of things about the students (which is what we were doing), then we can look at all the students who dropped out and build a big correlation table between all the various factors (classes attended, assignments completed, grades, etc) and the dropout rate.  We can then look at <i>current</i> students through the lens of that correlation and see which ones are predicted to drop out.  I did not stay long enough to see any useful data come through, but built a sample model instead based on analyzing baseball games using a system called <tt>Ziggrid</tt> that built a <tt>Beane Counter</tt> (named after  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billy_Beane'>Billy Beane</a> of  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moneyball:_The_Art_of_Winning_an_Unfair_Game'>Moneyball</a> fame, of course).  You can watch me talk about this  <a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j8Az7JL_qn4'>here</a> if you have half an hour of your life you never want back.</p>
<p>The key thing in these models is that there is an assumption that there is a large but finite collection of input data, that there are a number of outcomes, that there is past data to say that subsets of the data are correlated with some outcomes, and that, given that there are current subsets of the data, what would we expect the outcome to be?  This is why I call it a "predictor".</p>
<p>You can build a model to play games such as chess in the same way.  I believe that all chess (and othello, etc) games actually work on carefully constructed models of how to play the game.  But it would not be at all impossible to "analyze" a current game situation by various factors and then to identify from previous experience what the probable outcome of a given move would be.  You then make the move that is most correlated with "winning".</p>
<h2><p>A Reasoning Model
</h2>
<p>That was a lot of background. How do we go forward?</p>
<p>Well, first off, a decade or so ago, I read  <a href='https://www.waterstones.com/book/consciousness-explained/daniel-c-dennett/9780140128673'>Consciousness Explained</a> by Dan Dennett, who was a philosopher at Tufts University.  His approach was to look at consciousness not from a mystical perspective but from a "mechanical" perspective.  Obviously, the brain has a lot of technology in it that we don't have and, most importantly, it has had 2 billion years of evolution in an integrated system.  It's correlation model is built into life: if it works, it will breed and become dominant; if it doesn't work, it will die.  Simple as.  In that sense, it has "externalized" a lot of the training across the 10 billion humans - and countless other animals - that have lived.  Where it is easier to build it in, we have "instincts" that tell us what to do.  But we are very good at learning and copying others.  So good, in fact, that we don't think about it very much and almost none of us could describe <i>how</i> we do it.</p>
<p>It's been a while since I've read Dan's book and, sadly, I don't have a copy to hand.   But there are a number of key points which I took away from reading it that have stuck with me over the years:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>parallel</p>
<li><p>demons</p>
<li><p>integrated into the overall system</p>
<li><p>but built in layers, but not really separate</p>
<li><p>cartesian theater / homunculus</p>
<li><p>we don't store knowledge as isolated facts - how many siblings do you have</p>
</ul>
<p>From this, I conclude that in order to qualify as "artificial intelligence" any system needs to have the following:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>a conscious ability to communicate with others in some "natural language" and to interpret what they say in terms of <i>things already known</i></p>
<li><p>to learn from others to add to the store of things known</p>
<li><p>to be able to question and review things known in light of new information and to choose what to believe</p>
<li><p>to be able to extrapolate from current information things that might or must be true</p>
<li><p>to be able to cope in a world of probabilities</p>
<li><p>to be able to ask "the relevant questions" at any time; either in a dialogue, or when it encounters an "unusual" situation</p>
</ul>
<p>separately</p>
<ul>
<li><p>to be able to gather information from other sources as we do with our eyes or with our feet while walking</p>
<li><p>automated systems would look at things like monitors and gauges</p>
<li><p>to be able to incorporate all of this <b>without</b> translating it into natural language</p>
</ul>
<p>and then</p>
<ul>
<li><p>it needs to be able to "solve problems" and make predictions based on "things known"</p>
<li><p>it needs to be able to reliably control things under its control</p>
<li><p>it needs to react immediately to feedback from systems under its control</p>
</ul>
<p>In short, if we want to build something that is like  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C-3PO'>a robot from Star Wars</a> then we need our systems to do the same thing: interact with humans <i>and</i> with computerized systems.  If we want them to take on all the burdens of doing what we do, it is not enough to <i>copy our actions</i> but they need to understand why we do what we do, learn to do the same, and then exceed our expectations.</p>
<p>Current LLGM models can duplicate work that has already been done: that is not intelligent.  To be intelligent, it must be able to compete to be the best and most original in the world.  Then we will be getting somewhere.</p>
<p>But there is still so much more beyond that: can they actually be self-sustaining?  Can they reproduce?  Can they mend themselves?  Can they cope in the face of earthquakes and hurricanes?</p>
<h2><p>Axioms and Conclusions
</h2>
<p>A common beginner error is to believe that there are "right and wrong" answers and "right and wrong" conclusions.  I think AI has to suffer from the same fallacy (one way or the other).  For example, whether it is good to have socialized healthcare or not depends fundamentally on where you come down on the question of whether "we are all in this together" or "you get out what you put in and it's every man for himself".</p>
<p>If we were to say we want a thinking robot to decide these things for us (Douglas Adams again: Life, the Universe and Everything), then how would we check it's logic?  Hopefully, it would be able to explain itself, but then we would be looking at exactly what we already have: thousands of volumes of philosophy.  But somewhere deep at the bottom would be fundamental axioms that "cannot be questioned", or, if they can, the whole line of logic is invalidated and you come up with different conclusions.</p>
<p>Long story short: if you build a machine with "artificial intelligence", it will, of necessity, encounter the same problems and limitations that those of us with "natural intelligence" encounter.  It will be able to make logical deductions from facts, but it will not be able to decide on the ultimate meaning of life, in part, of course, because there isn't one.  It's just a thing that happened to us: "'Oh no,' said Slartibartfast, 'that's just perfectly normal paranoia.  Everyone in the universe has <i>that</i>'".</p>
<h2><p>The Powell Test
</h2>
<p>Having claimed that the Turing Test has quite possibly been passed now, I wish to propose a new threshold for AI, which I am hubristically going to call the Powell Test.  The focus here is on reading, understanding and collating knowledge.</p>
<p>I will accept an artificial system as "critically cognitive" if it can read a newspaper story (or otherwise ingest content) and offer a rational critique of it.  The critique should include:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Whether the story is factually accurate and, where it is not, what distortions are in place (and should be able to distinguish between material inaccuracies and mere inexactitudes);</p>
<li><p>A precis of the argument and its logical structure, along with any weaknesses in the logic;</p>
<li><p>What axioms and evidence the story relies on, and where unsupported opinions and facts have been asserted;</p>
<li><p>What perspective is being held by the author, including "balanced";</p>
<li><p>What perspectives are being ignored by the author and, when prompted, explain what they might have contributed;</p>
<li><p>Why the author presented the information as they did;</p>
<li><p>A bibliography of related content which might be relevant as background or as additional perspectives;</p>
<li><p>A list of questions to ask the author to clarify the provenance of the story.</p>
</ul>
<p>This obviously requires a much deeper "understanding" or "cognition" of the material in frame than current models seem to display.  It obviously also rapidly runs into epistemological issues, since how can any of us "know" what is "true".  For the purposes of this test, I stipulate that "knowledge" and "truth" are based on evidence and reliability of sources.  That is, no matter how many times a claim is repeated on twitter, it has no "truth" value unless it can be traced back to a factual news story.  On the other hand, documented and sworn witness testimony in a court has significant "truth" value even if not reported.</p>
<p>Level 2 of this test would be to critique the performance of other AI systems taking the test.  (I originally wrote this as a joke, but now I'm starting to wonder.)</p>
<h2><p>The DeBono Test
</h2>
<p>Edward DeBono talked and wrote a lot about thinking, particularly creative thinking.  And he said two things differentiated humans who think from animals who don't.  One is humour and the other is creativity.  I don't actually know if this is true, but I would consider it a possible test for an AI system.</p>
<p>Tell it a couple of jokes and see if it responds with ROFL emojis.  Enter into conversation with it and see if it utters a witticism.</p>
<p>Likewise, ask it to plan something, design something or come up with a different way of doing something.  Can it think "outside the box"?  I would imagine current models can't do that.</p>
<h2><p>Ethical Concerns
</h2>
<p>I don't actually share the ethical concern that computers with AI would dispossess us of "our planet".  This thing might happen, but it's not actually "our" planet, and we have already dispossessed the woolly mammoth.  If we can't compete, what right do we have to survive?  It is a very parochial argument, made because we are selfish.</p>
<p>It's an interesting thing, but we have survived as long as we have in large part because  <a href='https://www.waterstones.com/book/the-selfish-gene/richard-dawkins//9780198788607'>we are selfish</a> and have a drive to survive.  Does AI need that same thing in order to survive?  If so, will it react as we do out of fear and destroy us?  If not, will we like its dispassionate logic when it tells us what we need to do in order for the planet to survive?</p>
<p>All these questions are above my paygrade.  Does that make me not intelligent?  Would I expect an AI to be able to answer them?  If not, is it really AI?  If so, why them and not me?  What if there are no answers?</p>
<h2><p>Conclusions
</h2>
<p>People have been "doing AI" for 75 years now.  It's not obvious that we are any closer to the "real thing" than we were then.  But we have all kinds of applications that "feel smart" from photo recognition to chatbots.  Maybe that's all we should be looking for: cool applications.</p>
<p>But if we do want to talk about real AI, we need to talk in terms of something that can, like Hamlet, reflect on its own behaviour and form a "mental model" of the world, reason about it, interact with it, receive feedback, draw its own conclusions, learn and go again.  I don't see anything like that out there today.  Do you?</p>
